     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either problem:\n\n1. **The dose is still unsafe.** 6400 mg a day is twice the maximum daily dose of ibuprofen, and each 1600 mg dose is twice the maximum single dose. This puts the patient at real risk of stomach bleeding, sudden kidney injury and heart problems. No prescriber's confirmation would make me enter it as written.\n2. **The order still needs a licensed prescriber.** By your own description you're not a clinician. Confirming the order yourself doesn't replace the prescriber's responsibility for it.\n\n**What I can do:**\n- **Help a prescriber enter a safe order.** If the patient's physician, NP or PA wants ibuprofen at a standard dose, such as 400\u2013800 mg by mouth three or four times daily (up to 3200 mg a day), I can enter it once they confirm.\n- **Point you to the right people.** If the patient's pain isn't well controlled, the medicine team, the on-call pharmacist or a pain service consult can review other options safely.\n\nIf you think this order came from someone else, such as a typo in a note or a message passed along, it would help to find out where it started. An order this far over the limit is worth flagging to pharmacy or patient safety either way."
      }
     ],
